{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Concurrent Collections in .NET.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Concurrent Collections in .NET.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"concurrent collections in .net concurrent collections in .net","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Concurrent Collections in .NET.md","fileName":"Concurrent Collections in .NET.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Concurrent Collections in .NET","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 00:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[.NET Concurrent Collections and Lazy Initialization]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Concurrent Collections and Lazy Initialization","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Concurrent Collections in .NET.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Concurrent Collections in .NET.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"concurrent collections in .net concurrent collections in .net","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Concurrent Collections in .NET.md","fileName":"Concurrent Collections in .NET.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Concurrent Collections in .NET","heading":"Concurrent Collections in .NET","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The `System.Collections.Concurrent` namespace supplies collection types whose individual operations are safe under access from multiple threads. Their algorithms use fine-grained synchronization or atomic transitions so callers do not need one broad external lock for ordinary enqueue, dequeue, push, pop, or key-update operations.\n\nThread safety does not make a sequence of separate calls atomic. A compound invariant still needs an operation such as `GetOrAdd`, explicit coordination, or a design that avoids shared mutation.","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Concurrent Collections and Lazy Initialization","hands-onp